<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RQFlinch - shot mid-getup, compressed. Sibling of hitreaction-onfloor; the
  design rationale is in hitreaction/RQFlinch.xml and RQFlinch.lua.

  THE DOORWAY. getup-fromOnFront/to_hitreaction-gettingUp.xml has three
  transitions and the live one for gunfire is the second: <isTrue>hashitreaction</isTrue>
  plus <isTrue>bOnFloor</isTrue>, with no compare against any particular
  reaction string. So ANY hit landing while the zombie is on the floor and
  getting up face-down routes here - and getup-fromOnFront is the back half of
  our own compressed crit chain, entered from onground whenever fallOnFront is
  true. bOnFloor stays true until the getup node's SetOnFloor(false) fires at
  the halfway mark, which at 20x is a window of a frame or two rather than the
  half-second vanilla leaves open. Narrow, not closed.

  There is a second, engine-side doorway: CombatManager.java:2407 rewrites
  HitReaction.FLOOR to GETTING_UP_FRONT when the target is in ZombieGetUpState
  and isFallOnFront(). That one is the vehicle lane, since FLOOR is only ever
  written by a vehicle impact (IsoGameCharacter.java:11643) or bent fences
  (BentFences.java:362,370) - not by gunfire.

  WHAT IT COSTS UNCOVERED: vanilla's GettingUpFront emits KnockDown at Start -
  setOnFloor(true) and setKnockedDown(true) (ZombieHitReactionState.java:106-109)
  - and runs unscaled. So a shot landing in that window put a Bulwark back on
  the floor with the knockdown flag re-armed, which is exactly the outcome the
  feature exists to prevent.

  Exit is <eventOccurred>ActiveAnimFinishing</eventOccurred> -> onground, so
  speed scaling shortens it. Copied from vanilla GettingUpFront.xml, changed
  only by: our condition, an explicit priority, speed 20, zeroed blend, and
  KnockDown swapped for CancelKnockDown. FallOnFront, Collide and SetState are
  vanilla's, kept verbatim - FallOnFront(true) is what sends onground back into
  getup-fromOnFront rather than getup-fromOnBack.

  As in RQFlinchBack.xml, dropping KnockDown does not leave bOnFloor unset:
  it was already true (a condition of every transition that reaches here) and
  onground sets it again on entry (ZombieOnGroundState.java:41-43).

  ONE NODE, no condition beyond ours, because vanilla has exactly one
  non-ragdoll node in this state and it carries no conditions at all.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>RQFlinchGettingUpFront</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Zombie_OnKnees_ToFloorFront</m_AnimName>
	<m_Looped>false</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.0</m_BlendTime>
	<m_BlendOutTime>0.0</m_BlendOutTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>20.00</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_SyncTrackingEnabled>false</m_SyncTrackingEnabled>
	<m_ConditionPriority>100</m_ConditionPriority>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-6201-4000-8000-rftddirge6201">
		<m_Name>RQNoFlinch</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>true</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6202-4000-8000-rftddirge6202">
		<m_EventName>FallOnFront</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>true</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6203-4000-8000-rftddirge6203">
		<m_EventName>CancelKnockDown</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>true</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6204-4000-8000-rftddirge6204">
		<m_EventName>Collide</m_EventName>
		<m_TimePc>0.1</m_TimePc>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6205-4000-8000-rftddirge6205">
		<m_EventName>SetState</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
</animNode>
